Keep the filter graph when a VA-API decoder's new frames context is equivalent

fftools/ffmpeg_filter.c send_frame() rebuilds the whole filter graph
("Reconfiguring filter graph because hwaccel changed", logged at INFO and not
suppressed by -reinit_filter 0) whenever a frame arrives in a hw_frames_ctx
other than the one the input was configured with. With -hwaccel vaapi, hevc
calls get_format() on every in-band SPS change (hevcdec.c:3260), and
ff_get_format() begins with ff_hwaccel_uninit(), which drops the decoder's
frames context (decode.c:1256, 1226), so the first frame after the change
always carries a new one - even when device, surface format and size are
unchanged. Stranger Things Blu-ray remuxes switch SPS/PPS at the logo ->
programme IRAP (11.053s on S05E02) and again before the closing.

A rebuild frees the graph and configures a new one, so every stateful filter
restarts in the middle of the read: setpts=PTS-STARTPTS rebases to 0 again,
the input -t (a trim in the graph) counts its duration again from the new
graph's first frame, fps drops its buffered frame, and libvmaf/libvmaf_sycl
writes its log for the first segment before a second instance overwrites it
with the rest. Measured on the B580: av1_vaapi probe encodes failed with
AVERROR_BUG or emitted 77 frames for a 64-frame window, and a zero-copy score
over 84 frames kept 59. A software decode of the same file does not rebuild:
it has no frames context to change.

With this patch, a new context that is VA-API, on the same device, with the
same format, sw_format, width and height does not rebuild the graph: the input
takes a reference to the new context, logs "Keeping filter graph" at VERBOSE,
and the graph carries on. Every rebuild that still happens logs the same INFO
"Reconfiguring filter graph" line as before.

Why the configured graph can take frames from the new context (n9.0.1 lines):
- buffersrc uses its context only to set up its output link (buffersrc.c:560);
  its per-frame check compares size, format and colour, not the context
  (buffersrc.c:231).
- fps, trim, setpts, format and null never read hw_frames_ctx; a filter that
  is not hwframe-aware forwards its input link's context (avfilter.c:428-438).
- vaapi_vpp (scale_vaapi) takes each frame's surface (vaapi_vpp.c:555) and
  colour format from the frame's own context (vaapi_vpp.c:432); its VA context
  is created on its output surfaces (vaapi_vpp.c:230).
- VA-API encoders read the context once at open for device and sw_format
  (ffmpeg_enc.c:160, hw_base_encode.c:795) and use each frame's data[3] as the
  input surface (vaapi_encode.c:847, 590, 171).
- libvmaf_sycl (ffmpeg-n9.0.1-libvmaf-sycl-zerocopy.patch) takes the surface
  from data[3] and the display from each frame's own context; the bit depth it
  configures from sw_format is required to be equal.
- hwdownload was the one filter that required the configured context by
  pointer. The transfer dispatches on the frame's own context (hwcontext.c:
  487-489) and checks size against it (hwcontext_vaapi.c:977), so it accepts
  an equivalent context too, and says so once at VERBOSE ("Accepting frames
  from an equivalent hwframe context"). Without that hunk a VA-API read
  through hwdownload fails with EINVAL at the change and logs no rebuild line.

Not compared: initial_pool_size, which follows the new SPS (and is 0 for both
under libva >= 1, vaapi_decode.c:628), and the surface array, which no consumer
of decoder frames reads.

Conditions and limits:
- Still rebuilds, as before: a switch to or from software frames, another
  device or device type, a new coded size (even one that keeps the display
  size, which a software decode would not rebuild for) or sw_format (bit
  depth), and anything that already needs a rebuild for another reason: the
  test runs only while need_reinit is still 0. That includes a new display
  size (a conformance-window change, hevcdec.c:342-343), colour description
  or range (a VUI change). fftools compares those on the frame itself
  (ffmpeg_filter.c:3082-3088), so they rebuild a software decode too, and this
  patch does nothing for them. Before a graph is first configured nothing
  changes.
- QSV is excluded: qsvvpp binds its session to the input context's surface
  array (qsvvpp.c:579-587) and qsv_map_to looks surfaces up in the configured
  context (hwcontext_qsv.c:2188). A QSV decoder that reinitialises at a
  parameter change (qsvdec.c:827-829, 1022) allocates a new frames context
  too (qsvdec.c:327-355), and its graphs still rebuild with every stateful
  filter restarting; scdet, for one, has no previous picture for the first
  frame after the change (vf_scdet.c:114-136).
- Frames of the old context still inside a kept graph (fps's held frame,
  framesync's queue, an encoder's lookahead) are now synced after the decoder
  destroyed the VA decode context they came from; a rebuild used to free
  them. hevc already outputs DPB pictures after that destroy (get_format() at
  hevcdec.c:3260 runs before ff_hevc_output_frames() at 3312). In iHD
  (media-driver ff3fed1, softlet) such a sync is safe only when both of these
  hold:
  (a) The sync starts after vaDestroyContext has returned. SyncSurface reads
      the surface's pDecCtx with no lock (media_libva_interface_next.cpp:
      1377-1380), and the decode StatusCheck reads it before taking
      SurfaceMutex, dereferences it with no null check and calls into the
      codec HAL (ddi_decode_functions.cpp:1020-1023, 1048). DestroyContext
      tears that HAL down and deletes it before it clears pDecCtx, and takes
      no lock StatusCheck takes (ddi_decode_base_specific.cpp:452-464).
      This does not hold for every frame of the old context. Frames pushed
      on by frames of the new context are synced after get_format() has
      returned, but the up to 2 frames already queued between the decoder
      and filter threads (DEFAULT_FRAME_THREAD_QUEUE_SIZE) can be synced
      while the destroy is still running.
  (b) The surface is still in the destroyed context's render-target table:
      DestroyContext clears pDecCtx only there
      (ddi_decode_base_specific.cpp:458-465), and a full table reuses
      inactive slots without clearing theirs (ddi_codec_base_specific.cpp:
      94-107). The table has 127 entries (media_libva_common_next.h:43),
      which a decoder pool never approaches.
  The race at the change itself - the decoder thread destroying the context
  while the filter thread syncs the up to 2 frames queued between them - is
  not changed here, but reads that used to be rebuilt now keep running on
  the GPU across the change, so that race is exercised on every change a
  read crosses. It has been seen in production: zero-copy scores crossing
  the Stranger Things S05E03 and S05E04 closing SPS changes segfaulted iHD
  on fc0 (iHD_drv_video.so+0x90a61c) with compute and blitter engine resets.
  For SPS changes the VA-API hwaccel can decode as they are,
  ffmpeg-n9.0.1-hevc-keep-hwaccel-on-equivalent-sps.patch removes it: hevc
  no longer destroys the context there. Changes it still re-initialises
  keep the race.
- VRAM: the old context stays referenced by the graph's links and filters
  (buffersrc, hwdownload, vaapi_vpp, libvmaf_sycl) and by an encoder, and its
  pool destroys surfaces only when it is freed (hwcontext_vaapi.c:515-529).
  So the surfaces it allocated stay allocated beside the new pool until the
  command ends: one extra decoder pool per change a read crosses, estimated
  at 10-25 surfaces: about 0.23-0.58 GiB for 4K P010 at 3 bytes a pixel
  (about 0.12-0.3 GiB for 8-bit NV12). Not measured. A rebuild used to free
  it for -f null scoring graphs; an encoder already kept it.
- Verified offline only: applies at --fuzz=0 to n9.0.1 on its own and after
  the three other ffmpeg-n9.0.1 patches, and both files compile with no
  warnings. The behaviour above is from reading the source and still needs a
  run on the card. Line numbers are n9.0.1's; another FFMPEG_REF needs the
  patch re-cut.

--- a/fftools/ffmpeg_filter.c
+++ b/fftools/ffmpeg_filter.c
@@ -3061,6 +3061,46 @@
     return str ? str : "unknown";
 }
 
+/* Whether frames from hw frames context b can go through a filter graph that
+ * was configured for context a, so a change from a to b need not rebuild it.
+ *
+ * A hwaccel decoder allocates a new frames context every time get_format()
+ * runs, and hevc runs it on every in-band SPS change, including one that
+ * leaves the picture size and surface format as they were. Rebuilding the
+ * graph for that restarts every stateful filter in it mid-stream: the input
+ * -t trim, setpts=PTS-STARTPTS, fps, and a scorer writing its log.
+ *
+ * The graph does not need its frames to come from the context it was built
+ * with, as long as they are surfaces of the same kind on the same device:
+ * VA-API filters and encoders take the surface from each frame (data[3]) and
+ * the display from the frame's own hw_frames_ctx, and the old context stays
+ * referenced by the graph's links while the graph lives. VA-API only, because
+ * that is what was checked; QSV binds VPP sessions and derived mappings to a
+ * context's surface array. The pool size is not compared: it follows the new
+ * SPS's DPB, nothing downstream of a decoder allocates from its pool, and the
+ * old pool stays valid while the graph holds its context.
+ *
+ * Changes this does not cover still rebuild, in software decodes too: a new
+ * display size, colour description or range (a conformance-window or VUI
+ * change) differs in the frame itself, not only in its context. */
+static int hw_frames_equivalent(const AVBufferRef *a, const AVBufferRef *b)
+{
+    const AVHWFramesContext *fa, *fb;
+
+    if (!a || !b)
+        return 0;
+
+    fa = (const AVHWFramesContext *)a->data;
+    fb = (const AVHWFramesContext *)b->data;
+
+    return fa->device_ctx->type == AV_HWDEVICE_TYPE_VAAPI &&
+           fa->device_ctx       == fb->device_ctx &&
+           fa->format           == fb->format     &&
+           fa->sw_format        == fb->sw_format  &&
+           fa->width            == fb->width      &&
+           fa->height           == fb->height;
+}
+
 static int send_frame(FilterGraph *fg, FilterGraphThread *fgt,
                       InputFilter *ifilter, AVFrame *frame)
 {
@@ -3114,8 +3154,20 @@
         need_reinit = 0;
 
     if (!!ifp->hw_frames_ctx != !!frame->hw_frames_ctx ||
-        (ifp->hw_frames_ctx && ifp->hw_frames_ctx->data != frame->hw_frames_ctx->data))
-        need_reinit |= HWACCEL_CHANGED;
+        (ifp->hw_frames_ctx && ifp->hw_frames_ctx->data != frame->hw_frames_ctx->data)) {
+        if (!need_reinit && fgt->graph &&
+            hw_frames_equivalent(ifp->hw_frames_ctx, frame->hw_frames_ctx)) {
+            /* keep the graph, and hold the frames' own context: later frames
+             * then match by pointer again, and a rebuild for any other
+             * reason is configured with the context frames now come from */
+            ret = av_buffer_replace(&ifp->hw_frames_ctx, frame->hw_frames_ctx);
+            if (ret < 0)
+                return ret;
+            av_log(fg, AV_LOG_VERBOSE, "Keeping filter graph: input %s moved to "
+                   "an equivalent hw frames context\n", ifilter->name);
+        } else
+            need_reinit |= HWACCEL_CHANGED;
+    }
 
     if (need_reinit) {
         ret = ifilter_parameters_from_frame(ifilter, frame);
--- a/libavfilter/vf_hwdownload.c
+++ b/libavfilter/vf_hwdownload.c
@@ -33,6 +33,8 @@
 
     AVBufferRef       *hwframes_ref;
     AVHWFramesContext *hwframes;
+
+    int logged_equivalent;
 } HWDownloadContext;
 
 static int hwdownload_query_formats(const AVFilterContext *avctx,
@@ -111,6 +113,23 @@
     return 0;
 }
 
+/* The test fftools' send_frame() uses to keep a filter graph when a VA-API
+ * decoder replaces its frames context with one of the same device, surface
+ * format and size (an in-band SPS change). The download itself goes through
+ * the frame's own context, so such a frame downloads exactly as one from the
+ * configured context would. The first such frame logs one verbose line, which
+ * is also what a build can grep for to prove this hunk is compiled in. */
+static int hwframes_equivalent(const AVHWFramesContext *a,
+                               const AVHWFramesContext *b)
+{
+    return a->device_ctx->type == AV_HWDEVICE_TYPE_VAAPI &&
+           a->device_ctx       == b->device_ctx &&
+           a->format           == b->format     &&
+           a->sw_format        == b->sw_format  &&
+           a->width            == b->width      &&
+           a->height           == b->height;
+}
+
 static int hwdownload_filter_frame(AVFilterLink *link, AVFrame *input)
 {
     AVFilterContext *avctx = link->dst;
@@ -124,12 +143,21 @@
         err = AVERROR(EINVAL);
         goto fail;
     }
-    if ((void*)ctx->hwframes != input->hw_frames_ctx->data) {
+    if ((void*)ctx->hwframes != input->hw_frames_ctx->data &&
+        !hwframes_equivalent(ctx->hwframes,
+                             (AVHWFramesContext*)input->hw_frames_ctx->data)) {
         av_log(ctx, AV_LOG_ERROR, "Input frame is not the in the configured "
                "hwframe context.\n");
         err = AVERROR(EINVAL);
         goto fail;
     }
+    /* reached with a different context only if it passed the test above */
+    if ((void*)ctx->hwframes != input->hw_frames_ctx->data &&
+        !ctx->logged_equivalent) {
+        av_log(ctx, AV_LOG_VERBOSE,
+               "Accepting frames from an equivalent hwframe context.\n");
+        ctx->logged_equivalent = 1;
+    }
 
     output = ff_get_video_buffer(outlink, ctx->hwframes->width,
                                  ctx->hwframes->height);
